I did that too, except i put the file in ~/.vim/syntax/, so didn't need
to edit system-wide settings.
 
> What I'd change is using different colors for keywords (e.g. 'unlit' and
> strings (e.g. '"watchman"').

You can do that already; the syntax file doesn't define colors for
those, your color theme does.  Just define different colors for Constant and
String types in ~/.vimrc or change the color theme.

The only colors the syntax file defines are for the map characters inside
MAP-ENDMAP.

> Using a color for MONSTER/OBJECT/etc. that is
> better readable if you're on a "dark letters on bright background" window
> might be advantageous for general use.

Again, you just need to change the color theme. MONSTER/OBJECT/etc are
Keywords.

> Might also be nice to differenciate
> the keywords MONSTER, OBJECT, TRAP, etc. by different colors (but that's a
> matter of taste).

Hm.  Currently MONSTER/OBJECT/TRAP/etc are all Keywords, so their color
is defined by the color theme, but if you can think of a good way to do that..

